Original research
-----------------

This project is an independent implementation inspired by Pool Party
process injection research by Alon Leviev (SafeBreach Labs), presented
at Black Hat EU 2023 under the title "The Pool Party You Will Never
Forget: New Process Injection Techniques Using Windows Thread Pools."

  - https://github.com/SafeBreach-Labs/PoolParty
  - https://www.safebreach.com/blog/process-injection-using-windows-thread-pools/

The TP_DIRECT.Callback hijack primitive, the TP_IO / TP_ALPC / TP_JOB
object kinds, and the NtSetIoCompletion dispatch trigger all originate
from the Pool Party research. The specific primitive implemented here
(walking a victim's live TP_POOL.PoolObjectList, hijacking an existing
pool object's embedded TP_DIRECT.Callback in place rather than inserting
a new TP_<kind> structure, with a per-run restore stub) is not one of
the eight originally published variants.

Thread pool internal structure layouts (TP_POOL, TPP_CLEANUP_GROUP_MEMBER,
TP_IO, TP_ALPC, TP_JOB, TP_DIRECT) were reconstructed from WinDbg "dt"
output against ntdll shipped with Windows 10 and Windows 11 builds listed
in InternalStructures.h.

